se it builds in the review tree; the suites above stand in for it.\n- rebase (self): every one of the 16 rebased commits is `=` in range-diff, so no conflict was resolved inside a 0307 commit. Against origin/main, src/go/check.go differs only in help text, and 0308's removal of the timestamp warning stands (check.go:167). src/go/check_test.go has zero removed lines. The 0308 bullet that docs/erg-manual.md deleted is not back. tickets/.claims/0308 is one line whose slug matches tickets/closed/0308-*.erg. Both the head build and the vendored tickets/erg pass `erg check tickets/`.\n- redteam, consider: src/go/install.go:176. If copying HEAD's blob into the git dir fails (disk full, unwritable git dir), the hook clears erg_bin and falls through to the index-only check. A merge that brings a claimed duplicate number then passes. Not reproduced, and no ordinary user state triggers it, so it is below the bar. The fix would be to refuse when a tracked executable blob exists but cannot be prepared. The same applies to the mirror in src/go/assets/integration.md.\n- correctness, consider: src/go/install.go:186. The hook always runs HEAD's binary. If the incoming side brings a corpus in a format that binary rejects, the merge is refused with \"corpus check failed\", and nothing hints that the binary is stale. This is the safe direction, `git commit` concludes the merge, and v1's closed header set makes such a change a spec-version event.\n- correctness, consider: src/go/install.go:191. The notice \"it changes in this merge, or is a symlink\" gives the wrong reason in two cases: after a failed blob copy, and for a tracked non-executable (100644) tickets/erg. This is wording only.\n- rebase, consider: tickets/.claims/0308:1. origin/main has no .claims/, so any ticket that lands on main before this PR merges will be unclaimed on the merge result. The pre-merge rebase must rerun `erg migrate --claims tickets/` as dfb2dd4 did, or main's CI goes red.\n\nUnresolved simplify: not plann